#---
# purpose: "negation inside a guard works for floats as well as integers, on every door"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# # negate_guard
#
# A guard is a third place negation is implemented, after the expression
# evaluator and native codegen. It forced its operand to an integer, so a float
# guard silently FAILED -- falling through to the next clause rather than
# erroring:
#
#     defp classify(x :: float) when -x > 0.0, do: :negative
#     classify(-5.5)   interp :other   run :negative   build :negative
#
# Wrong answer, no error, one door. It stayed invisible while native codegen
# aborted on the same input (fz-5xp.33): there was nothing for interp to
# disagree with until that was fixed.
#
# ## What it covers
#
# Negation in a guard over floats and integers, both branches, and a guard
# whose operand is a union so no door can decide the lane statically. The
# clause heads are type-annotated, which is fz's spelling of Elixir's
# `is_float`/`is_integer` guards -- without them the first clause claims every
# integer too, because `-(-5) > 0.0` is true in both languages.
defp classify(x :: float) when -x > 0.0, do: :negative_float
defp classify(x :: integer) when -x > 0, do: :negative_int
defp classify(_), do: :other

defp pick(0), do: -4.5
defp pick(_), do: -9

def main() do
  dbg(classify(-5.5))
  dbg(classify(5.5))
  dbg(classify(-5))
  dbg(classify(5))
  dbg(classify(pick(0)))
  dbg(classify(pick(1)))
end
